(9.3 Numerical Feature Scaling: StandardScaler) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 637.7955118110235162 Td
(The 8 continuous numerical features differ significantly in scale and units. For example, `person_income` ranges in tens) Tj
T* (or hundreds of thousands of dollars, whereas `loan_percent_income` is a decimal ratio between $0.00$ and $1.00$.) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 596.4096850393700606 Td
(Unscaled features can cause gradient-based algorithms \(such as Logistic Regression\) to oscillate and converge slowly,) Tj
T* (as weights for large-magnitude features dominate the cost function.) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 555.023858267716605 Td
(To standardize numerical features, Scikit-Learn's **`StandardScaler`** was applied. For each continuous feature $j$,) Tj
T* (the transformation computes:) Tj
ET
0.96 g
56.6929133858267775 533.480551181102328 481.8941732283464034 -19.8425196850393704 re
f
BT
/F6 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.078 g
216.6399999999999864 522.1419685039370506 Td
(z = \\frac{x - \\mu_j}{\\sigma_j}) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 499.4648031496063254 Td
(where $\\mu_j$ represents the empirical mean and $\\sigma_j$ represents the standard deviation calculated exclusively) Tj
T* (on the training partition. The fitted scaler was subsequently applied to transform test and inference vectors.) Tj
ET
BT
/F10 12 Tf
13.7999999999999989 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 441.0711023622047264 Td
(9.4 Categorical Feature Encoding: OneHotEncoder) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 424.0632283464566967 Td
(Machine learning estimators require numerical input matrices. The 5 categorical features \(`person_gender`,) Tj
T* (`person_education`, `person_home_ownership`, `loan_intent`, and `previous_loan_defaults_on_file`\) are qualitative) Tj
T* (nominal variables without intrinsic ordinal ranking.) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 369.0711023622047264 Td
(Using integer label encoding \(e.g., $1, 2, 3$\) would falsely impose mathematical hierarchy \(e.g., implying that) Tj
T* (Education category 3 is greater than category 1\). Therefore, **`OneHotEncoder`** was employed.) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 327.685275590551214 Td
(To ensure robustness against previously unseen categories during web inference, the encoder was initialized with:) Tj
ET
0.96 g
56.6929133858267775 319.7482677165353948 481.8941732283464034 -19.8425196850393704 re
f
BT
/F6 9 Tf
10.3499999999999996 TL
0.078 g
130.2399999999997817 308.4096850393700606 Td
(\\text{OneHotEncoder}\(\\text{handle\\_unknown} = \\text{"ignore"}\)) Tj
ET
BT
/F9 10 Tf
11.5 TL
0.118 g
56.6929133858267775 285.7325196850392786 Td
(When an unrecognized category is encountered during live inference, the encoder generates an all-zero vector across the) Tj
T* (respective category columns rather than raising a runtime exception.) Tj
ET
